  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nAs of 2 PM Sunday afternoon, a weak midlevel low remains across the \nRed River Valley. The best forcing for ascent has remained across \nOK, which has kept the greatest coverage of rain showers across OK \nas well. Aside from a stray sprinkle this afternoon, dry conditions \nshould prevail for the remainder of the day. The persistent cloud \ncover has held temperatures in the 40s for most locations. \nTransitioning into tonight, the aforementioned midlevel low will \nprogress into east TX with midlevel ridging spreading into the \nPlains. A LLJ will strengthen across portions of central and western \nKS tonight. This should allow overcast skies to continue with the \npotential for low clouds across central KS. Temperatures will be \nslow to cool overnight with the overcast skies. Overnight lows are \nforecast to range from the the upper 30s to the lower 40s. \n\nFor Monday and beyond, a lee surface trough across the high Plains \nwill deepen Monday into Tuesday as a western CONUS trough approaches \nthe central US. The increasing surface pressure gradient will result \nin southerly wind gusts up to 45 mph Tuesday and Wednesday. Latest \nglobal models have trended further west with the track of the \nshortwave trough passage late Tuesday night into Wednesday. As a \nresult, the prospects for rainfall continue to decrease with mostly \ndry conditions now expected. The main trough axis is poised to eject \ninto the Plains late next week into the weekend and may result in \nrain chances returning to the area. The arrival of this trough is \nlikely to yield gusty south winds up to 40 mph on Friday. In regards \nto temperatures, the persistent southerly flow throughout the week \nwill promote a warming trend will temperatures gradually warming \neach day. High temperatures for the second half of the week are \nlikely to reach the 60s to near 70.",
